Confirmed against `ProcessOperationInvoker.invokePromise` (`packages/core/compute/compute-runtime/src/ProcessOperationInvoker.ts:325`) — it wraps the run in try/catch and returns `{ error }`, so a handler failure never reached my `catch`, and `rejected` was ignored outright.

Fixed in `d2031166`: the handler now reads the resolved value and sets `failed` when `error` is present or `data.rejected` names this plugin; the `catch` stays for an unexpected rejection.

```ts
const { data, error } = await invokePromise(RegistryOperation.EnablePlugins, { ids: [pluginId] });
if (error || data?.rejected.some(({ id }) => id === pluginId)) {
  setFailed(true);
}
```

Not done: a component test for the rejected case. This container needs a plugin-manager host, and the story harness cannot register a plugin while leaving it disabled — the one state this component exists for — so there is no honest way to assert it yet; I'd rather note the gap than add a test that fakes the state. The operation's own `rejected` path is covered in `enable-plugins.test.ts` ("rejects a plugin the host does not have installed").

On the JSDoc nitpick: both symbols already carry one — `PluginPrompt.tsx:18-22` and `PluginManagerSkill.ts:23` — so nothing to change there.

