body:
Done, and applied to the whole interface rather than just `spawn`. `RemoteProcessManager` no longer names a `ProcessProtocol` type anywhere: the wire shapes are the transport's business, so decoding them is the implementation's job and a consumer of this interface never sees them.

Every verb takes an options bag with the space in it:

```ts
spawn(request: SpawnRequest): Effect.Effect<Snapshot>;                 // { spaceId, key, name?, parentPid?, environment?, annotations? }
list(request: ListRequest): Effect.Effect<readonly Snapshot[]>;        // { spaceId, key?, target?, state? }
status(target: ProcessTarget): Effect.Effect<Snapshot>;                // { spaceId, pid }
submitInput(target: ProcessTarget & { input: unknown }): Effect.Effect<void>;
makeRpcClient(target: ProcessTarget & { group: RpcGroup<Rpcs> }): Effect.Effect<RpcClient<Rpcs>, never, Scope>;
terminate(target: ProcessTarget): Effect.Effect<void>;
readEvents(target: ProcessTarget & { cursor: number }): Effect.Effect<EventPage>;
```

The domain types it returns instead: `Snapshot extends Process.Info` with one added field (`alarmDueAt`, the one thing a remote caller cannot otherwise know), an `Event` union, and `EventPage { events, cursor, truncated, snapshot }`. `spaceId` is a branded `SpaceId`, `pid` a `Process.ID`.

The wire decoding moved to `@dxos/edge-compute/process-snapshot.ts` (`decodeSnapshot`, `decodeEvent`, `toSpawnRequest`) — next to the client that produces the bytes. `RemoteProcessHandle` dropped its own decode path with it, so a handle is a view over already-decoded domain values.

Since then this interface also gained the manager verbs the deleted adapter used to hold, in the same shape — `spawn({ spaceId, key, definition?, ... })`.

