You're right to correct me, and I want to be explicit about it: **the TextSelector predicate does exist** and my "simply not in the code" was wrong. It's at 1019-1030:

```ts
...items.filter((item) => {
  if (spaces.includes(item.spaceId)) {
    return true;
  }
  if (item.queueId) {
    return queues.some((queueRef) => {
      const queueId = extractQueueRefs([queueRef])?.[0]?.queueId;
      const spaceId = extractSpaceIdFromQueue(queueRef);
      return queueId === item.queueId && spaceId === item.spaceId;   // <-- here
    });
  }
  return false;
})
```

I read the queue-item construction, saw it taking `spaceId` off the row, and stopped — the filter is ~15 lines further on, past where I looked. I then stated as fact something I hadn't checked. That's the error, not the conclusion.

The conclusion does survive: `extractSpaceIdFromQueue(queueRef)` is the same call on the same input as before this PR, so for an unqualified URI it yields `undefined`, `undefined === item.spaceId` is false, and the item was dropped before this PR exactly as it is after. Agreed it's pre-existing and agreed it's a reasonable follow-up — worth someone deciding whether an unqualified feed URI should resolve against any space or none, since right now it half-does: the SQL admits the row and this filter discards it.

Thanks for going back to the base revision to settle it rather than taking my word.

