body:
Checked this against the convention and I don't think it holds — leaving the comment as is.

The guideline is that a **comment** ends with a period, not that every wrapped `//` line does. This comment is a single sentence soft-wrapped across three lines, with the period on the last one. Its immediate neighbours in the same file are written the same way:

```ts
// One host yield per module keeps activation tasks under the long-task threshold;
// counted as wait, not run, by the split below.          // ← 342-343

// Wait/run split: `module:<id>` spans the whole pipeline, so scheduling delay (in-flight
// providers + requires resolution) is indistinguishable from work without these sub-measures.   // ← 345-346

// Cap activation so a single misbehaving module can't hold the
// event chain open. On timeout the failure is recorded against
// the plugin and surfaced as `PluginTimeoutError`.        // ← 356-358
```

Applying the rule per-line would require breaking each of those into sentence fragments too.

The proposed replacement also moves the wrong way on a second guideline: `CLAUDE.md` asks for the constraint in **one load-bearing clause, not a multi-sentence essay**, and the suggestion splits one sentence into two.

Flagging one thing in your own review info, since it may explain this: *"CodeRabbit could not clone the repository, so clone-backed analysis was skipped and this review may be incomplete."* Without the clone the surrounding comments in this file weren't visible, which is exactly the context that settles the convention here.


