`teardown()` sets `stopped = true` as its first statement, so once a failed `start` has run it, a subsequent `stop()` hits the `if (stopped) return;` guard and **`onStop` never fires**.

That matches the new doc comment on `start` ("after tearing down without `onStop`"), but it means the shutdown signal is unavailable to anyone who calls `stop()` expecting to get it — and `stop()` is documented as "run `onStop`. Idempotent.", which now isn't quite true after a failed start.

Either track it separately:

```ts
let onStopPending = true;
// ...
const stop = () => Effect.promise(async () => {
  try {
    if (!stopped) { await teardown(); }
  } finally {
    if (onStopPending) { onStopPending = false; await onStop?.(); }
  }
});
```

…or note on `stop()`'s doc comment that it is a no-op, `onStop` included, once `start` has failed.

