body:
Fixed in `66234239` — and my earlier reply on this thread was wrong, so correcting it.

You were right that the parked write has to be settled on close. What I got wrong was the mechanism of the breakage I blamed it for. Two separate mistakes in my attempt:

1. I **rejected** the promise. Resolving is correct — the chunk is dropped because nothing will ever consume it, and the pipe should end cleanly rather than error.
2. I settled it by invoking `#subscribeCb`, whose closure runs `#popFrames()`. That dereferences `this.#messageCb!`, which at close time does not exist, so it threw. That throw, not the settling, is what broke `going offline and back online`.

The fix stores the resolver separately:

```ts
#settlePendingWrite(): void {
  this.#subscribeCb = undefined;
  const resolve = this.#pendingWriteResolve;
  this.#pendingWriteResolve = undefined;
  resolve?.();
}
```

called from `#handleClosed`. `memory-transport.test.ts` is 8 passed / 1 skipped, stable across repeated runs, and `going offline and back online` passes.

There is no reconnect precondition after all — a related claim of mine that measurement had already disproved. The real hazard was the one you described: a pending write stranding the `pipeTo` feeding the framer, which hung the transport's `close()`.

