Added in 0a43bdfe:

```ts
export const layer = <const Tags extends readonly Context.Key<any, any>[]>(opts: {
  readonly layers: LayerSpec.LayerSpec[];
  readonly services: Tags;
}): Layer.Layer<Service, never, Context.Service.Identifier<Tags[number]>>
```

`LayerStack.Service` is the tag for the built stack; the layer picks the declared tags out of its context (`Context.pick`) so the stack sees exactly what the type promised, and destroys the stack when the layer's scope closes. Two tests cover it: ambient services arriving through the layer context, and teardown on scope close.

The type safety is the point — an ambient requirement that goes missing is currently invisible, since a spec whose requirement is absent is pruned rather than failing. This PR has one bug that was exactly that shape (subduction enabled in config with no edge endpoint pruned `DataSpaceManager` and the six rpc services built on it), so the compile error is worth having.

`makeClientServicesStack` does not use it yet: its ambient set includes the edge clients, which exist only with a configured endpoint, and `services: Tag[]` is all-or-nothing. Making those specs rather than ambient services would fix that — happy to do it here or as a follow-up, whichever you prefer.

