body:
Fixed in 935356ae. You're right — the early return contradicted the method's own documented guarantee ("resolves once the pushed entities are queryable"). An identical snapshot arriving after another call had folded the same change into the buffer but before that call scheduled its pass would return without awaiting anything, and its caller could query rows the indexer had not written yet.

Every accepted call now awaits `updateIndexes()`; only a changed snapshot schedules a new run:

```ts
const changed = await this._acceptRegistrySnapshot(clientId, entries, opts);

if (changed) {
  this.#scheduleIndexRun('registry-update');
}

// Awaited even when nothing changed: an identical snapshot can land between another client's
// buffer update and the pass that indexes it, and returning early here would let this caller
// query rows the indexer has not written yet.
await this.updateIndexes();
```

The `_ctx.disposed` early-out above it stays — a closed host has nothing to index against.

Regression: `registry-indexing.test.ts` 24/24, `index-core` 65, `echo-host` 383 + the known pre-existing `automerge-repo-subduction` flake (passes in isolation, fails a different test each run on a clean tree), `echo-client` 587. `pnpm format` and lint clean.

