body:
Done in efc67f70. `QueryPlanner` takes the path as constructor config and owns the compile decision:

```ts
export type QueryPlannerOptions = {
  defaultTextSearchKind: QueryPlan.TextSearchKind;
  /** Evaluation path `QueryPlanner.compilePlan` targets. */
  executor?: QueryExecutorMode;
  noIndexes?: boolean;
};

compilePlan(query): Effect<QueryPlan.Plan, QueryError | SqlError, SqlClient>
```

`compilePlan` builds the steps, declines a plan that reads `@meta`, and otherwise returns a plan whose single `SqlStep` stands for them. `QueryExecutorMode` and `resolveQueryExecutorMode` moved here too, since the path is now planning-time config; everything outside imports them from `query/index.ts` as before, so no call site changed.

One thing I did not do, and want to flag rather than bury: `createPlan` stays pure and synchronous. Compiling reads the store — `metaVersion` literal sets come from `objectSnapshot` and the day boundaries of a named zone from `objectMeta` — so folding it into `createPlan` would have made it effectful, and the compiler itself calls `createPlan` recursively for subqueries (`compile.ts:604`, `compile.ts:1325`), as does `_resolveInQueryFilter`. Making it effectful would have rippled through those recursions and all ~20 planner tests for no gain. So compiling is a second method, and the executor resolves it once on first execution rather than in its constructor. Say the word if you want it folded in anyway.

